adjudication.rationale:
In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after `safeParse`, the code explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

So when the refresh response lacks `refresh_token` (which is expected in many OAuth refresh flows), it mutates the parsed data to contain the literal placeholder string. Call sites then use this parsed data to update stored credentials (e.g., in `office365calendar/lib/CalendarService.ts` it merges `tokenResponse.data` into `o365AuthCredentials` before persisting via `prisma.credential.update`), which would overwrite a real refresh token with the placeholder. This is a concrete correctness defect introduced by the diff.

The handler decrypts and parses the incoming `keys` without any error handling:

```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
```

Both `symmetricDecrypt(...)` (e.g., wrong/missing `CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` due to `|| ""`, invalid ciphertext) and `JSON.parse(...)` (non-JSON plaintext) can throw. There is no surrounding `try/catch` and no `safeParse` equivalent, so an invalid payload or misconfiguration will bubble up as an unhandled exception and return a generic 500 response from Next.js. This is directly verifiable from the diff.

In apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts the auth check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is unset (i.e., `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is `undefined`) and the request omits the header (so `req.headers[...]` is also `undefined`), the `!==` comparison evaluates to `false` and the request is accepted, allowing unauthenticated calls on a misconfigured deployment. The comparison is also a normal equality check rather than a constant-time compare for a secret value.

In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts` the secret check is:
```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```
If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is truly unset (i.e., `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET === undefined`) and the request omits the header (so `req.headers["calcom-webhook-secret"] === undefined`), then the condition becomes `undefined !== undefined` which is `false`, so the request is not rejected and proceeds unauthenticated. This is a real bypass introduced by the diff. The claim about missing tests is not verifiable from the diff, but the bypass itself is.
